NAME
    cheat - manage RAM pokes and ROM patches

SYNOPSIS
    cheat add <code> [description]
    cheat poke <space> <addr> <value> [description]
    cheat gg <code> [description]
    cheat rompatch <addr> <value> [<compare>|-] [description]
    cheat list
    cheat master [on|off]
    cheat enable <id>
    cheat disable <id>
    cheat remove <id>
    cheat clear
    cheat save <name>
    cheat load <name>
    cheat files

DESCRIPTION
    Unifies two SNES-specific cheat mechanisms under one command: RAM pokes
    (Pro Action Replay/Game Wizard style) and ROM-read patches (Game Genie
    style). 'add' decodes an 8-character code, guessing which format it is
    from its punctuation (best-effort, not a guarantee - use 'gg' or 'poke'
    directly if the guess is wrong). 'poke'/'rompatch' add a cheat directly
    without code decoding, e.g. for an address already found with 'search'.
    'enable'/'disable' toggle a cheat without removing it.

    Some consoles' codes are wider than a byte or longer than a line - the
    N64 GameShark's 16-bit writes, tests and repeaters. 'add' takes such a
    code whole and makes ONE cheat of all its lines. The shell splits on
    spaces, so join the lines with '+' and leave the space out of each:
    'cheat add D00002000005+810001001234 lives'. A line the console's
    format will not apply is refused with the reason, not stored.

THE MASTER SWITCH
    'cheat master off' silences every cheat at once; 'cheat master on'
    brings them back. It is a SECOND AXIS, not a bulk edit: it does not
    touch any individual cheat's own enabled flag, so switching off to
    check whether a cheat is causing a bug and switching back on returns
    exactly the arrangement you had, however many were on. A cheat added
    while it is off is inert until it is switched back on. 'cheat list'
    says so at the top while it is off, because otherwise every [on] in
    that listing would be a lie.

    It is runtime state and is NOT saved. 'cheat save' does not write it
    and 'cheat load' does not set it: a set saved months ago with the
    switch off would otherwise silently kill every cheat in a session that
    had it on. It starts on, which costs nothing - everything imports
    disabled anyway, so nothing is applying until you say so.

    'save'/'load' persist a named set to /etc/EmuSen/cheats/<name>.json - see
    'man hier'. That file is ordinary JSON with addresses and bytes written
    as hex text, so it can be written or corrected by hand from this shell;
    an entry whose hex doesn't parse is skipped and reported, and the rest of
    the file still loads. 'load' ADDS to whatever is already loaded rather
    than replacing it - run 'cheat clear' first to replace. 'files' lists the
    saved sets. Nothing is loaded automatically: a cheat set applies only
    when you ask for it, so a saved file can't silently alter a later run.

THE WRITE MODEL
    A cheat is one description, one enable flag, and a LIST of writes. That
    matters because real cheats are rarely one address: 'max every item' is
    a run of twenty, and they have to arm and disarm together. One cheat,
    one ID, one toggle, however many addresses it drives - 'cheat list'
    prints a header line and then one indented line per write.

    Each write carries:

        width         1, 2 or 4 bytes. A wide write covers consecutive
                      addresses.
        byte order    little-endian unless the write says otherwise. Only
                      meaningful above one byte.
        type          set, increase or decrease. Increase/decrease read what
                      is there and adjust it, so they accumulate every frame
                      - 'increase by 1' climbs, it does not hold at 1.
                      Or a TEST - if-equal / if-not-equal - which writes
                      nothing: the next write in the cheat that is not a
                      test happens only if it passes, and a run of tests
                      must all pass. 'cheat list' prints one as 'if ... =='.
        bit position  makes the write touch a single bit instead of whole
                      bytes, leaving the other seven alone. For flag bytes
                      where poking the whole byte would clobber unrelated
                      state.
        repeat        count, address stride and value stride. One write can
                      cover a whole run: 8 repetitions stepping the address
                      by 2 pokes every other byte of an inventory table.

    Two combinations are refused rather than half-implemented. A ROM patch
    cannot increase or decrease - the substitution happens at read time and
    the real byte is never written, so there is nothing to accumulate into.
    And a compare byte only works on a single-byte ROM patch, because the
    read hook is handed one byte at a time and cannot check a compare that
    spans several addresses; allowing it would give a torn patch where the
    first byte declines and the rest apply.

IMPORTING RETROARCH CHEATS
    'cheat import <path.cht>' reads RetroArch/libretro .cht files - the
    format the libretro cheat database ships in, one file per game. Both
    shapes a .cht can take are handled:

        cheatN_code       the core-native code string, hex, with several
                          codes joined by '+' becoming one multi-write cheat.
                          Decoded through the same code decoder 'cheat add'
                          uses, so the per-system layout is never hardcoded.
        cheatN_address    RetroArch's own handler fields - cheat_type,
                          memory_search_size, address_bit_position,
                          big_endian, repeat_count and the two strides. These
                          are DECIMAL in a .cht file, unlike the code string.

    memory_search_size 3/4/5 are 1/2/4-byte writes; 0-2 are sub-byte and
    become bit writes at address_bit_position. cheat_type 0 means the entry
    exists but does nothing, and is skipped rather than imported as a no-op.
    An entry that cannot be decoded is skipped and counted; the rest of the
    file still imports.

    Everything imports DISABLED. A database file routinely holds dozens of
    cheats and switching them all on at once is never what anyone meant -
    'cheat list' then 'cheat enable <id>' picks the ones you want.

    'cheat export <path.cht>' writes RAM pokes back out in RetroArch's
    handler form, which round-trips every field above. ROM patches are
    skipped and counted: RetroArch's model has no ROM-read substitution, so
    there is nothing honest to write for them. A cheat containing a test
    is skipped and counted too: one handler entry holds one write, and a
    test with the write it guards dropped would be a different cheat.

THE CHEAT DATABASE
    'cheat db' works over a DIRECTORY TREE of .cht files - one file per
    game, in per-system folders, which is exactly how RetroArch stores its
    cheats. EmuSen ships no cheat data of its own and redistributes none.
    There are two ways to have a database, and neither involves us hosting
    anything:

        Use one you already have. Set AppSettings.CheatDatabaseDirectory to
        an existing RetroArch cheats folder and it is indexed as-is - no
        copying, no conversion. Unset, it defaults to home/Cheats (see
        'man hier').

        'cheat db update' downloads one. It fetches the same archive
        RetroArch's own Online Updater does, straight from libretro to your
        machine, on your explicit request. The data is licensed CC BY-SA 4.0;
        the codes themselves were aggregated from community sources,
        substantially GameHacking.org, and credit belongs to their original
        authors. That notice is printed with the result rather than buried
        here, because attribution is a condition of the licence.

    Only .cht entries are extracted, and an archive entry that tries to
    write outside the target directory is skipped rather than followed.

    'cheat db' with no argument reports where the database is and how many
    files per system. 'find' searches it; 'load' imports the best match,
    disabled, the same way 'import' does. Matching tolerates a loaded ROM's
    file name - 'cheat db load Super Mario World (USA).sfc' finds
    'Super Mario World (USA).cht' - and prefers an exact name over a prefix
    over a substring, so a game whose title is a prefix of another still
    wins for its own name.

    In EmuSen.Mistress the same thing is on the menu bar at Settings > Cheat
    Database..., which shows the folder, what is installed per system, the
    attribution notice, and a download button. Selecting a system there
    lists that system's games (with a filter box - a real SNES folder holds
    several thousand), and picking one loads its cheats into the active
    list, disabled, the same way 'cheat db load' does. It REPLACES that
    list rather than adding to it, unlike the command: picking the same
    game twice from a list is an ordinary thing to do and must not double
    every cheat, whereas typing the command twice is not. An Active
    Cheats... button sits next to Close, since turning a list on is what
    anyone does next.

    Settings > Active Cheats... is the GUI half of 'list', 'enable',
    'disable', 'remove', 'clear' and 'master' - one checkbox per cheat, the
    master switch, and a box to type a code into using the same format
    guess 'add' makes. That list is owned by the frontend rather than by
    the running core, so it survives a Reset and can be built before any
    ROM is loaded; loading a DIFFERENT ROM clears it, since one game's
    addresses mean nothing in another. See EmuSen_Settings_Reference.md
    section 4.14.

    Its Apply Cheats button is not an arm/commit step - ticking a box
    already takes effect on the next frame, the same as 'cheat enable'.
    Apply forces one poke immediately (useful while paused, where no next
    frame is coming) and reports how many landed; it also turns the master
    switch back on, since applying cheats that the switch would swallow is
    not what the button says.

    Save Cheat List writes the list under the running game's file name,
    the same store 'cheat save' uses - so 'cheat load <game>' finds it
    from the shell. Mistress reloads it by itself the next time that game
    starts, which is the point: a saved list is not re-imported from the
    database every session. It never overwrites a list already in hand, so
    a Reset or a close-and-reopen keeps whatever was edited since. See
    section 4.15.

PRUNING THE DATABASE
    The libretro database ships around 44 systems and a build with one
    core can use one of them, so most of a 250MB download is dead weight
    that every database scan still walks. `cheat db prune` deletes the
    system folders no core in this build claims.

    It is core-agnostic: each core declares the cheat-database folder
    names it covers (CoreDescriptor.CheatSystems), and the pruner keeps
    those and drops the rest - so a second core is one registry entry
    rather than an edit to the pruner. Venus claims both the main SNES
    folder and Satellaview, which is the same cartridge hardware.

    Deleting is irreversible and the only way back is another 250MB
    download, so a bare `cheat db prune` only ever lists what would go;
    `--apply` is what actually deletes. It refuses outright rather than
    emptying the database in the two cases that would: when no core
    claims any system at all, and when nothing on disk matches anything
    claimed (a wrong folder, or a wrong mapping). In Mistress the same
    thing is the Prune Unsupported button, whose first click reports and
    whose second deletes; a finished download offers it, since that is
    when the 250MB actually lands. See section 4.16.

EXAMPLES
    cheat poke WRAM 9c 63 infinite lives
    cheat list
    cheat disable 1
    cheat master off
    cheat save zelda
    cheat clear && cheat load zelda
    cheat import "Super Mario World (USA).cht"
    cheat export my-cheats.cht
    cheat db update
    cheat db find mario
    cheat db prune
    cheat db prune --apply
    cheat db load Super Mario World (USA)
